  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on self-reported subjective health as the primary outcome measure, which may be prone to different reporting biases across age groups.",
      "what": "This study tracked 162 Swiss adults (64 younger and 98 older) over 15 days, analyzing 1,106 person-days of GPS and accelerometer data to measure active-mobility green space exposure (AMGSE) during walking and cycling. Using the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), the researchers found no correlation between static home-neighborhood greenness and active-travel greenness. Mixed-effects ordinal logistic regression models revealed that higher overall active-travel greenness between individuals was positively associated with daily subjective health (beta = 0.894), and that daily fluctuations in this exposure had a stronger positive association with subjective health in younger adults (beta = 0.079).",
      "why": "The study demonstrates that static neighborhood greenness measures do not accurately reflect the actual green space exposure people experience during active travel. It also establishes that the daily health benefits of traveling through green spaces vary significantly by age group, suggesting that urban greening strategies must account for active transit corridors rather than just residential buffers."
    }
  },
